  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\nResearch Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\nAbstract\n========\n\nThe rapid advancement of code-generating AI agents has revolutionized software development,\nenabling automated code synthesis, debugging, and deployment. However, these powerful systems\nalso introduce significant security risks, as they can be manipulated through adversarial\ntechniques to produce malicious or erroneous code. This paper reviews recent research advances\nin the field of code agents and the emerging discipline of red-teaming—systematic evaluation\nof model safety through adversarial probing. We discuss the unique challenges posed by code\nagents, including prompt injection vulnerabilities, supply chain attacks via automated PRs, and\ndata exfiltration through generated code. Furthermore, we examine methodologies for red-teaming\ncode agents, drawing on established frameworks for evaluating the trustworthiness of large\nlanguage models. We identify key research directions for developing more robust and secure\ncode agents, emphasizing the need for rigorous adversarial evaluation frameworks.\n\n1. Introduction\n===============\n\nCode agents represent a class of AI systems capable of understanding, generating, and\nmodifying source code autonomously. Recent advances in large language models have led to\nthe development of sophisticated code agents that can perform complex software engineering\ntasks, from implementing new features to identifying and patching security vulnerabilities.\nWhile these capabilities offer tremendous potential for accelerating software development,\nthey also introduce novel attack vectors that can be exploited by malicious actors.\n\nThe trustworthiness of AI systems has become a critical concern as these models are\ndeployed in increasingly sensitive applications. Research such as DecodingTrust: A\nComprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models [1] has highlighted the\nvarious dimensions of trust