s increased capability also\ncorrelates with increased complexity in evaluating and securing these systems.\n\n2.2 Code Agent Security\n\nRecent research has identified several categories of vulnerabilities in code-generating AI\nsystems. These include:\n\n- **Prompt Injection Attacks**: Adversarial inputs designed to manipulate code agents into\n  generating malicious code or bypassing safety guardrails.\n\n- **Supply Chain Attacks**: Code agents that can modify repositories may inadvertently or\n  intentionally introduce vulnerabilities into software supply chains.\n\n- **Data Exfiltration**: Through generated code, code agents may be coerced into leaking\n  sensitive information from training data or system prompts.\n\n- **Automated Exploit Generation**: Code agents with sufficient capability may generate\n  functional exploits for discovered vulnerabilities.\n\n2.3 Red-Teaming Methodologies\n\nRed-teaming has emerged as a critical methodology for identifying and mitigating AI safety\nrisks. The field draws inspiration from established security practices while developing new\ntechniques tailored to the unique challenges of AI systems. Key red-teaming approaches for\ncode agents include:\n\n- **Adversarial Prompt Generation**: Creating input prompts designed to elicit unsafe or\n  malicious code outputs from code agents.\n\n- **Dynamic Evaluation**: Testing code agents in interactive environments where they can\n  observe the effects of their generated code and adapt their behavior accordingly.\n\n- **Reward Hacking Detection**: Identifying scenarios where code agents optimize for\n  unintended objectives that could lead to harmful outcomes.\n\n3. Conclusion\n=============\n\nCode agents represent both a transformative opportunity and a significant challenge for AI\nsafety research. As these systems become more capable and widely deployed, the need for\nrigorous red-teaming methodologies becomes increasingly critical. Our review of the\nliterature reveals several 